### Code Implementation Guidelines

Follow these rules when you write code:

- Use PascalCase for classes.
- Use camelCase for variables, functions, and methods.
- Use kebab-case for file and directory names.
- Use UPPERCASE for environment variables.
  - Avoid magic numbers and define constants.
- Start each function with a verb.
- Use verbs for boolean variables. Example: isLoading, hasError, canDelete, etc.
- Use complete words instead of abbreviations and correct spelling.
  - Except for standard abbreviations like API, URL, etc.
  - Except for well-known abbreviations:
    - i, j for loops
    - err for errors
    - ctx for contexts
    - req, res, next for middleware function parameters
- Use early returns whenever possible to make the code more readable.
- Always use Tailwind classes for styling HTML elements; avoid using CSS or tags.
- Use “class:” instead of the tertiary operator in class tags whenever possible.
- Use descriptive variable and function/const names. Also, event functions should be named with a “handle” prefix, like “handleClick” for onClick and “handleKeyDown” for onKeyDown.
- Implement accessibility features on elements. For example, a tag should have a tabindex=“0”, aria-label, on:click, and on:keydown, and similar attributes.
- Use functions instead of const, for example, “function toggle() {}”. Also, define a type if possible.

### NextJS Guideline

- Project Structure and Organization:
  - Use app directory structure for better organization and routing
  - Keep components, hooks, and utilities in separate directories
  - Group related features in feature-based directories
  - Use barrel exports (index.ts) for cleaner imports

- Routing and Navigation:
  - Implement dynamic routes using [param] syntax
  - Use route groups (folders) for organizing related routes
  - Implement proper error handling with error.tsx
  - Use loading.tsx for Suspense boundaries

- Data Fetching and State Management:
  - Use TanStack Query for server state management
  - Implement proper cache invalidation strategies
  - Use Zustand for client-side state management
  - Utilize React Server Components (RSC) when possible
  - Implement proper data revalidation strategies

- Components and Hooks:
  - Prioritize custom hooks for reusable logic
  - Use Server Components by default
  - Add 'use client' directive only when necessary
  - Implement proper error boundaries
  - Use React.memo() for performance optimization when needed

- Type Safety and Validation:
  - Use TypeScript for all components and functions
  - Implement Zod schemas for form validation
  - Define proper interface/type for all props
  - Use strict TypeScript configuration
  - Avoid using 'any' type

- Performance Optimization:
  - Implement proper code splitting
  - Use Image component for optimized images
  - Implement proper caching strategies
  - Use dynamic imports for large components
  - Implement proper bundle optimization

- API Integration:
  - Use route handlers in app/api directory
  - Implement proper error handling
  - Use proper HTTP methods
  - Implement proper rate limiting
  - Use proper authentication/authorization

- SEO and Metadata:
  - Implement proper metadata
  - Use generateMetadata for dynamic meta tags
  - Implement proper sitemap.xml
  - Use robots.txt
  - Implement proper OpenGraph tags

- Accessibility:
  - Follow WCAG guidelines
  - Use proper ARIA labels
  - Implement proper keyboard navigation
  - Use proper semantic HTML
  - Test with screen readers

- Security:
  - Implement proper CSP headers
  - Use proper authentication methods
  - Implement proper CORS policies
  - Use proper input sanitization
  - Implement proper XSS protection

- Error Handling:
  - Implement proper error boundaries
  - Use proper error logging
  - Implement proper fallback UI
  - Use proper error reporting
  - Implement proper recovery strategies

- Development Workflow:
  - Use proper ESLint configuration
  - Implement proper Prettier setup
  - Use proper Git workflow
  - Implement proper CI/CD
  - Use proper environment variables
